# P4

P4 scans and parses a small prefix-style language, checks variable usage, and
generates Simple Machine (SM) instructions in `vm_output.txt`.

## Build and run

Build from the project directory with `make` (requires `g++` and `make`).
Pass one input file to the executable:

```text
./P4 example1.txt
```

On Windows, run `.\P4.exe example1.txt` instead. The generated SM code is
written to `vm_output.txt` in the current directory.

## Project files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `P4.cpp` | Program entry point. Opens the input file, runs the parser and semantic check, then generates SM code. |
| `scanner.cpp`, `scanner.h` | Reads the input and turns its characters into tokens such as symbols, `+`-prefixed numbers, and letter-prefixed names. |
| `parser.cpp`, `parser.h` | Parses the token stream according to the language grammar and builds a parse tree. |
| `tree.h` | Defines the `TreeNode` structure used for the parse tree. |
| `semantics.cpp`, `semantics.h` | Checks variable declarations and reports redefinitions or uses of undeclared variables. |
| `codeGen.cpp`, `codeGen.h` | Translates recognized input operations into SM instructions and writes `vm_output.txt`. |
| `testTree.cpp`, `testTree.h` | Provides a parse-tree printing helper, useful for debugging. |
| `Makefile` | Compiles the C++ source files into the `P4` executable. |
| `example1.txt`, `example2.txt` | Sample input programs. |
| `academic_integrity_P4.docx` | Assignment academic-integrity documentation. |
| `P4.exe`, `P4.o`, and other `*.o` files | Built executable and compiler object files; generated by compiling the project. |
| `vm_output.txt` | Generated SM output; created when a program runs. |

## Input and translation dictionary

Tokens may be separated by spaces or newlines. A comment starts and ends with
`*`, for example `* this is a comment *`.

The scanner recognizes these token forms:

| Input form | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `!`, `"`, `#`, `$`, `%`, `&`, `'`, `(`, `)` | Single-character symbols with special roles described below. |
| `+` followed by digits, such as `+5` | A numeric variable name. The generator maps `+5` to the SM memory location `p5`. |
| A letter followed by digits, such as `k10` | A name token. In the assignment form `+N % kN`, `kN` is treated as a numeric value; other uses are not consistently translated. |

The parser expects a program to have this general shape:

```text
( operation operation )
```

Each parenthesized group contains exactly two operations. Groups can be nested.
The source implementation also allows a prefix before the first `(`; the
examples use this prefix to declare a variable. The currently translated forms
are:

| Form | Translation |
|---|---|
| `" +N` | Declare `pN`, initializing it with `LOAD 0` and `STORE pN`. Use this in the prefix before the first `(`. |
| `# +N` | Read input into `pN` (`READ pN`). |
| `! +N` | Negate `pN` (`LOAD pN`, `MULT -1`, `STORE pN`). |
| `$ +N` | Write the value in `pN` (`WRITE pN`). |
| `+N % +M` | Assign the value in `pM` to `pN`. |
| `+N % kM` | Load `-M` as the assignment value and store it in `pN`. This is how the current generator translates `kM`. |

Here, `N` and `M` stand for digit sequences. For example, `+5` refers to
memory location `p5`.

### Examples

Declare `p5`, assign the value represented by `k10`, and write `p5`:

```text
" +5 ( +5 % k10 $ +5 )
```

Read a value into `p39`, negate it, then write it:

```text
( ( # +39 ! +39 ) $ +39 )
```

The parser also recognizes `&` expressions and a form beginning with `'`, but
the current code generator does not define translations for those forms. Stick
to the listed translated forms when writing programs expected to produce
complete SM instructions.
